Aight, lemme break it down real quick and show ya code. We need the smallest number made only of 4s and 7s with same count of each, and that number gotta be >= n. Them numbers always got even length (2k), with k 4s and k 7s. So what we do: for k = 1 up, build the string with k '4's and k '7's, generate every permutation of that string (use next_permutation), turn each into a number, and keep the smallest one that's >= n. Stop once we scanned enough lengths to find an answer. That approach easy and fast cause combinations stay small (C(2k,k) small for our k range). Below C++ code use cin/cout like problem said.

